/*****************************************************************************
    regProbe -- THE POP FOR THE DORMANT `register` ATTRIBUTE. 2026-08-03.

    ⚠ DORMANT PRIOR ART GETS A FIXTURE BEFORE IT GETS LOAD. `register` predates
    the members gate, addingMembers, currentDefine and this year's registry
    work. It was heavily used long ago and dormant since, so "it used to work"
    is a claim about a different language than the one running today.

    THE MECHANISM, read from Commands.rtn rEGISTER: as a noPrint definition
    attribute it takes item.parent as the argument, then

        if name  registri = registries[name];      <- attribute carries data
        else     registri = currentRegistry;
        registri += argument;

    ============================================================================
    ⚠⚠ THE TOPOLOGY IS LOAD-BEARING, AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS FIXTURE GOT IT
    WRONG. Recorded, because the correction is the finding.

    Draft 1 defined the subject and the probing action IN THE SAME REGISTRY.
    Check B then reported that an UNREGISTERED member was findable by bare
    name -- which reads exactly like "the members gate has drifted and Tony's
    pollution fix is compromised", the one finding this brief says outranks
    everything and means tools down.

    IT WAS THE FIXTURE. A same-registry lookup does not go through the search
    list at all, so it could never have shown the gate working. The real case
    is CROSS-REGISTRY: gXpress lives in `Generating`, and the caller that went
    dark reaches it through the search list. Re-measured against the real
    specimen (incant/sweepProbe) before raising any alarm: gXpress is STILL
    dark, so the gate is intact.

    So the subject lives in RegSubject (a registry ON the search list) and the
    prober lives in RegProbe. That is the topology the sweep actually cares
    about, and the only one under which B can mean what it claims.

    THE LESSON, and it is the one this project keeps paying for: an alarm is a
    causal claim, and a causal claim gets checked against the real specimen
    BEFORE it is raised. One command separated "the gate drifted" from "my
    fixture is shaped wrong."
    ============================================================================

    THREE CHECKS, and CHECK B IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE.

      A  a member-depth entry WITH `register`  -> bare locate() FINDS it
      B  the same entry WITHOUT `register`     -> bare locate() STAYS DARK
      C  both stay reachable through the parent that owns them

    ⚠ If A passes and B ALSO finds its entry -- under THIS topology -- then the
    gate really has drifted: tools down, report to Tony and Clay, do not
    proceed to Step 3.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (standing harness rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

/*  The SUBJECT registry -- mirrors `Generating`, which is where the members
    that went dark actually live.  */
register(RegSubject);
define
    holder
        regDark code={ print "regDark body ran":; };
        regLit register code={ print "regLit body ran":; };
    ;

/*  The PROBER registry -- a DIFFERENT one, so every lookup below is forced
    through the search list exactly as the real callers are.  */
register(RegProbe);
define
    probeReg code={
        a := regLit;
        if a.taG;
            print "A ok    regLit FOUND by bare name, tag =" a.taG:;
        else
            print "A FAIL  regLit NOT found by bare name -- register did not publish it":;
        b := regDark;
        if b.taG;
            print "B FAIL  regDark FOUND by bare name -- ⚠ THE GATE HAS DRIFTED, TOOLS DOWN":;
        else
            print "B ok    regDark stays dark (the gate still gates)":;
        c := holder["regDark"];
        if c.taG;
            print "C ok    holder[regDark] still reachable through its parent":;
        else
            print "C FAIL  holder[regDark] lost -- register/gate disturbed ownership":;
        };
    ;

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities RegSubject list;

probeReg();
print "REGPROBE SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
